✅ Structured Answer: Design a Notification System
1️⃣ Start with Definition (1–2 lines)

“A notification system is responsible for delivering messages to users via channels like push, email, SMS, or in-app alerts.
It is used for events such as order updates, OTPs, promotions, and reminders.”

2️⃣ Use Cases (Shows Practical Thinking ⭐)

“Typical use cases include transactional alerts (OTP, payments), engagement notifications (likes, comments), and marketing campaigns.”

✅ 3️⃣ Functional Requirements

✔ Send notifications
✔ Support multiple channels (Push / Email / SMS)
✔ Real-time & batch notifications
✔ Respect user preferences
✔ Retry on failures

✅ 4️⃣ Non-Functional Requirements

✔ Low latency (especially OTP)
✔ High availability
✔ Scalability
✔ Reliability (no message loss)

✅ 5️⃣ High-Level Architecture (Core Part 🎯)
4

“At a high level:”

🟢 Event Producers

Order Service

Payment Service

Social Service

👉 Generate events, not notifications directly

🟢 Message Queue (Kafka / RabbitMQ)

✔ Buffers spikes
✔ Decouples services
✔ Enables retries

🟢 Notification Service (Brain)

✔ Consumes events
✔ Checks preferences
✔ Personalizes content
✔ Chooses channel

🟢 User Preference Service

✔ Opt-in / Opt-out
✔ Channel selection
✔ DND settings

🟢 Template Service

✔ Stores templates
✔ Supports personalization

🟢 Delivery Services

Separate services:

✔ Push
✔ Email
✔ SMS

🟢 Third-Party Providers

✔ Firebase (Push)
✔ SendGrid (Email)
✔ Twilio (SMS)

✅ 6️⃣ Scaling Strategy (Q1)

“To scale:”

✔ Queue absorbs burst traffic
✔ Consumers scale horizontally
✔ Kafka partitions enable parallelism
✔ Delivery services scale independently

✅ 7️⃣ Ordering Strategy (Q6)

“Ordering is required only for certain flows like OTP or payment updates.”

✔ Use Kafka partition key = userId
✔ Kafka guarantees order within partition

✅ 8️⃣ Reliability & Failures

✔ Retry with exponential backoff
✔ Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)
✔ Fallback provider

✅ 9️⃣ Storage Decision (Q9)

“We store notifications for:”

✔ Auditing
✔ Debugging
✔ Analytics
✔ Retry tracking

Use:

✔ NoSQL / Data Lake

✅ 🔟 Effectiveness Metrics (Q10)

✔ Open Rate
✔ Click-Through Rate (CTR)
✔ Delivery success rate
✔ Latency

Tracking via:

✔ Pixels / Click links / App events

✅ 🎯 Final Summary (Strong Closing)

“Overall, I’d design the system using an event-driven architecture with Kafka, horizontally scalable consumers, user preference checks, template-based personalization, and channel-specific delivery services.
The system is optimized for scalability, reliability, and low latency.”

